{"name":"Shadow Cliff","hex":"#7a6f66","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"shadow","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A dark image projected onto a surface where light (or other radiation) is blocked by the shade of an object.","Relative darkness, especially as caused by the interruption of light; gloom; obscurity."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Unofficial, informal, unauthorized, but acting as though it were.","Having power or influence, but not widely known or recognized."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/shadow"},{"term":"cliff","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A vertical (or nearly vertical) rock face.","A point beyond which something abruptly fails or decreases in value, performance, etc."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Obsolete form of clef."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/cliff"}],"entries":[{"title":"Shadow","blocks":[{"text":"A shadow is a dark area on a surface where light from a light source is blocked by an object. In contrast, shade occupies the three-dimensional volume behind an object with light in front of it. The cross-section of a shadow is a two-dimensional silhouette, or a reverse projection of the object blocking the light."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ef/The_shadow_of_a_musician_playing_transverse_flute.jpg/330px-The_shadow_of_a_musician_playing_transverse_flute.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shadow","wikidata":"Q160020"},{"title":"Cliff","blocks":[{"text":"In geography and geology, a cliff or bluff or rock face is an area of rock which has a general angle defined by the vertical, or nearly vertical. Cliffs are formed by the processes of weathering and erosion, with the effect of gravity. Cliffs are common on coasts, in mountainous areas, escarpments and along rivers. Cliffs are usually composed of rock that is resistant to weathering and erosion. The sedimentary rocks that are most likely to form cliffs include sandstone, limestone, chalk, and dolomite. Igneous rocks such as granite and basalt also often form cliffs."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/White_Cliffs_of_Dover_02.JPG/330px-White_Cliffs_of_Dover_02.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cliff","wikidata":"Q107679"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/Park_grid.JPG/500px-Park_grid.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/Park_grid.JPG/1280px-Park_grid.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/Park_grid.JPG/500px-Park_grid.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Park fence shadow is distorted by an uneven snow surface.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APark_grid.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b4/Cumulus_cloud_shadows_1.jpg/500px-Cumulus_cloud_shadows_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b4/Cumulus_cloud_shadows_1.jpg/1280px-Cumulus_cloud_shadows_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b4/Cumulus_cloud_shadows_1.jpg/500px-Cumulus_cloud_shadows_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Shadows from cumulus clouds thick enough to block sunlight","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACumulus_cloud_shadows_1.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/85/Three_moons_and_their_shadows_parade_across_Jupiter.jpg/500px-Three_moons_and_their_shadows_parad